1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,767
Imagine a 68-year-old man in Delhi. It's early November.

2
00:00:04,767 --> 00:00:09,098
The smog has been bad for a week, and the nights are getting colder.

3
00:00:09,548 --> 00:00:14,158
His breathing is a little faster than usual. His oxygen is a point lower.

4
00:00:14,158 --> 00:00:18,705
He's reaching for his blue inhaler more often. None of it feels serious.

5
00:00:19,155 --> 00:00:22,906
Three days later, he's in hospital with a COPD flare-up.

6
00:00:23,356 --> 00:00:24,883
The warning signs were there.

7
00:00:24,883 --> 00:00:29,409
But nobody was watching them, and nobody connected them with the air he was breathing.

8
00:00:29,859 --> 00:00:34,236
Hi! We're team MOMENTUM. I'm Anushka Tomar, the team leader.

9
00:00:34,686 --> 00:00:41,073
And I'm Yash Saxena. We're both MBA students at SDA Bocconi Asia Center in Mumbai.

10
00:00:41,523 --> 00:00:44,929
This is BreatheTwin: a digital twin of a COPD patient.

11
00:00:44,929 --> 00:00:49,533
It learns each person's own normal, watches their city's air and weather,

12
00:00:49,533 --> 00:00:52,875
and warns one to three days before a likely flare-up.

13
00:00:52,875 --> 00:00:56,155
And it tells you why, and what would lower the risk.

14
00:00:56,605 --> 00:01:00,713
We'll walk you through the problem, how the twin works, and a live demo.

15
00:01:00,713 --> 00:01:04,423
Then we'll be honest about what our results do, and don't, prove.

16
00:01:07,123 --> 00:01:11,284
So, what is COPD? Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.

17
00:01:11,284 --> 00:01:15,816
It's a long-term lung disease that makes every breath harder.

18
00:01:15,816 --> 00:01:20,646
In 2016, India had an estimated 55 million people living with it.

19
00:01:21,096 --> 00:01:25,668
And here's what makes India different. The biggest causes aren't cigarettes.

20
00:01:25,668 --> 00:01:29,638
Outdoor air pollution drives about a third of India's COPD burden.

21
00:01:29,638 --> 00:01:35,293
Smoke from cooking with wood or dung drives another quarter. Both count for more than smoking.

22
00:01:35,743 --> 00:01:38,208
The most dangerous moment is the flare-up:

23
00:01:38,208 --> 00:01:41,200
a few days when breathing suddenly gets much worse.

24
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:46,247
Flare-ups put people in hospital, and each one can leave the lungs permanently weaker.

25
00:01:46,697 --> 00:01:50,338
But here's the hopeful part. The body gives warning signs.

26
00:01:50,338 --> 00:01:56,867
Breathing rises by about two breaths a minute two days before a flare-up, and about four the day before.

27
00:01:56,867 --> 00:02:02,140
Oxygen dips. Heart rate climbs. People walk less, and use their rescue inhaler more.

28
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:05,343
The trouble is, these changes are tiny.

29
00:02:05,343 --> 00:02:10,495
A family won't notice them, and nobody tracks them between clinic visits.

30
00:02:10,495 --> 00:02:16,777
Nobody links them to the smog, either. In the year we replay, Delhi's PM2.5 averaged 104.

31
00:02:16,777 --> 00:02:19,882
That's about seven times the WHO safe level.

32
00:02:20,332 --> 00:02:25,003
So the opportunity is clear. Let's use the days of warning the body already gives us.

33
00:02:27,703 --> 00:02:33,419
So how does it work? BreatheTwin brings together three streams of data for every patient.

34
00:02:33,869 --> 00:02:35,659
One: the health record.

35
00:02:35,659 --> 00:02:42,585
Age, how severe the COPD is, past flare-ups, smoking, cooking smoke at home, blood tests.

36
00:02:42,585 --> 00:02:45,542
That sets each person's starting risk.

37
00:02:45,992 --> 00:02:49,898
Two: daily readings from devices people can already buy.

38
00:02:49,898 --> 00:02:55,409
A smartwatch, a fingertip pulse oximeter, and a clip-on sensor for the inhaler.

39
00:02:55,859 --> 00:02:58,956
Three: the city's air quality and weather.

40
00:02:58,956 --> 00:03:06,696
And this part is real data, from CPCB monitoring stations in eight Indian cities, and Open-Meteo weather.

41
00:03:07,146 --> 00:03:12,760
Every morning, the twin compares today with the patient's own normal from the past month.

42
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:14,968
Not a textbook range. Their normal.

43
00:03:14,968 --> 00:03:20,646
Because a breathing rate of 18 can be fine for one person, and a warning sign for another.

44
00:03:21,096 --> 00:03:23,189
The risk engine is a hybrid.

45
00:03:23,189 --> 00:03:29,994
A machine-learning model, LightGBM, learns the patterns in the body signals and the record.

46
00:03:29,994 --> 00:03:33,583
For air pollution, we don't let the model guess.

47
00:03:33,583 --> 00:03:37,247
We use the effect from a published meta-analysis:

48
00:03:37,247 --> 00:03:41,809
about 2.5 percent more risk for every 10 micrograms of PM2.5.

49
00:03:42,259 --> 00:03:48,937
Then it decides. High: call the patient today. Medium: watch closely. Low: no action.

50
00:03:48,937 --> 00:03:51,294
And on top sits a safety rule.

51
00:03:51,294 --> 00:03:57,657
If oxygen falls below 90 percent, the patient is Urgent, whatever the model says.

52
00:03:57,657 --> 00:04:00,172
We'll show you that in a minute.

53
00:04:02,872 --> 00:04:09,547
Okay, let's open the dashboard. This is what a doctor or a community health worker sees each morning.

54
00:04:09,547 --> 00:04:15,761
We're replaying a real year of Indian air and weather, and today is the 5th of November, 2019.

55
00:04:15,761 --> 00:04:17,678
Winter smog is just starting.

56
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:23,493
Every page starts with a short guide, because we wrote this for people without medical training too.

57
00:04:23,980 --> 00:04:32,933
Across 420 patients in eight cities, 18 are high risk today. And Patna has the worst air: PM2.5 of 248.

58
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:34,411
That's very poor.

59
00:04:34,907 --> 00:04:41,054
The map shows the same picture by city. Bigger bubble, more high-risk patients. The colour is today's air.

60
00:04:41,537 --> 00:04:46,824
But this is the part a doctor will use most: who to call today, ranked by risk.

61
00:04:46,824 --> 00:04:49,902
Mohan Gowda, 92, in Lucknow, is at 94 percent.

62
00:04:49,902 --> 00:04:53,850
His breathing is 3.3 breaths a minute above his own normal.

63
00:04:53,850 --> 00:04:57,931
So before picking up the phone, the doctor already knows why.

64
00:04:58,388 --> 00:05:00,078
You can filter by age.

65
00:05:00,078 --> 00:05:07,224
Our patients range from 22 to 92, and every patient under 30 has a known cause of early COPD,

66
00:05:07,224 --> 00:05:12,141
like an alpha-1 gene variant, or lung damage after tuberculosis.

67
00:05:12,610 --> 00:05:16,755
Further down: smog and cold warnings for the next three days.

68
00:05:16,755 --> 00:05:23,756
Lucknow, Patna and Delhi expect very poor air this week. So the advice is practical. Keep windows shut.

69
00:05:23,756 --> 00:05:27,290
Wear an N95 outdoors. Keep the rescue inhaler close.

70
00:05:27,754 --> 00:05:30,655
And this chart shows why air matters across a season.

71
00:05:30,655 --> 00:05:35,745
As pollution climbs into winter, the number of high-risk patients climbs right along with it.

72
00:05:38,904 --> 00:05:43,883
Let's open one patient from that list. Vimala Pillai, 91, from Delhi.

73
00:05:44,355 --> 00:05:51,635
This dial is her chance of a flare-up in the next three days. 46.5 percent. Yesterday, it was under 3.

74
00:05:51,635 --> 00:05:58,416
The black mark is the alert line, and she's well past it. The twin's advice: contact her today.

75
00:05:58,896 --> 00:06:04,111
Why? Look at her readings against her own normal. Breathing:

76
00:06:04,111 --> 00:06:11,585
17.8 breaths a minute, that's 3.4 above her usual. Resting heart rate: 9 beats higher.

77
00:06:11,585 --> 00:06:16,626
And she walked about 1,900 steps, against her usual 3,100.

78
00:06:17,125 --> 00:06:23,757
These checks are simple rules a doctor can verify at a glance. Breathing up. Heart rate up.

79
00:06:23,757 --> 00:06:28,786
And she's taken only 64 percent of her daily inhaler doses this week.

80
00:06:29,244 --> 00:06:36,315
So what would help? Staying indoors with an air purifier brings her risk from 47 down to 40 percent.

81
00:06:36,315 --> 00:06:42,891
Taking every inhaler dose barely moves it today, because the warning signs are already there.

82
00:06:42,891 --> 00:06:46,214
We'd rather say that honestly than overpromise.

83
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:53,051
This is her health record, the fixed part of the twin. GOLD stage 2. One flare-up last year.

84
00:06:53,051 --> 00:06:59,609
A former bidi smoker. Cooking smoke at home. High eosinophils. Plus diabetes and heart disease.

85
00:06:59,609 --> 00:07:04,579
Together, that nearly doubles her risk compared with an average patient.

86
00:07:05,075 --> 00:07:10,465
And this chart breaks the risk into its causes. Red pushes it up, green pushes it down.

87
00:07:10,465 --> 00:07:13,006
Today, the biggest push is her breathing.

88
00:07:13,472 --> 00:07:18,892
Finally, the family can download a one-page visit summary and take it to the doctor.

89
00:07:18,892 --> 00:07:21,473
Readings, reasons, and questions to ask.

90
00:07:24,651 --> 00:07:29,904
Now, a case that shows why the model doesn't get the last word.

91
00:07:29,904 --> 00:07:36,407
Sunita Ansari, 30, from Delhi, with severe COPD. It's the 27th of March, 2019.

92
00:07:36,893 --> 00:07:44,553
The machine-learning model gives her less than 1 percent risk. On its own, that means: do nothing.

93
00:07:44,553 --> 00:07:51,040
But look at her oxygen this morning. 89.9 percent. In COPD, below 90 is a red flag.

94
00:07:51,540 --> 00:07:53,267
So the safety rule steps in.

95
00:07:53,267 --> 00:07:58,202
She's marked Urgent, and she goes straight to the top of the doctor's call list,

96
00:07:58,202 --> 00:08:00,115
with the reason in plain words.

97
00:08:00,606 --> 00:08:03,647
And honestly? We found this gap ourselves.

98
00:08:03,647 --> 00:08:07,775
When we stress-tested the twin on real hospital patients,

99
00:08:07,775 --> 00:08:15,740
someone with oxygen at 82 percent still came out Low. We didn't hide it. We fixed it. Rules catch emergencies.

100
00:08:15,740 --> 00:08:18,999
The model catches the slow drift before them.

101
00:08:22,174 --> 00:08:28,012
This is the what-if lab. A doctor, or a worried family member, can ask questions here safely.

102
00:08:28,012 --> 00:08:30,021
Nothing changes the real record.

103
00:08:30,491 --> 00:08:36,146
What if Vimala's readings went back to her normal, and she took every dose? Watch the dial.

104
00:08:36,146 --> 00:08:37,575
Her risk drops sharply.

105
00:08:38,042 --> 00:08:42,057
And what if Delhi had a week of severe smog instead? The risk goes up.

106
00:08:42,057 --> 00:08:45,441
That's the published pollution effect at work, not a guess.

107
00:08:45,907 --> 00:08:47,627
A cold snap pushes it up too.

108
00:08:49,383 --> 00:08:51,785
You can change the health record as well.

109
00:08:51,785 --> 00:08:56,883
Higher eosinophils, a blood marker, mean more frequent flare-ups. So the risk edges up.

110
00:09:02,084 --> 00:09:08,651
These curves change one thing at a time. The dot is where she is today. And they show the rules we built in:

111
00:09:08,651 --> 00:09:13,638
warning signs never lower the risk, and taking more inhaler doses never raises it.

112
00:09:13,638 --> 00:09:16,800
So every answer moves the way a doctor would expect.

113
00:09:19,972 --> 00:09:23,734
Now let's test the model live, on a patient it has never seen.

114
00:09:23,734 --> 00:09:26,525
This page builds a new twin in about a minute.

115
00:09:26,987 --> 00:09:34,959
Our test patient is a 23-year-old woman in Mumbai. GOLD stage 2. Three flare-ups last year.

116
00:09:34,959 --> 00:09:42,143
She's never smoked. Eosinophils of 320, a BMI of 19, and she's on a daily inhaler.

117
00:09:42,639 --> 00:09:44,019
Now her readings.

118
00:09:44,019 --> 00:09:52,542
Usually, she breathes 14 times a minute, her oxygen is 99 percent, and she walks about 4,000 steps a day.

119
00:09:52,542 --> 00:09:54,571
Over the last three days:

120
00:09:54,571 --> 00:10:02,607
18 breaths a minute, oxygen at 97, only a thousand steps, and one extra puff of her rescue inhaler.

121
00:10:03,068 --> 00:10:11,004
Honestly, none of those numbers looks alarming on its own. Her oxygen is still 97. So, what does the twin say?

122
00:10:11,460 --> 00:10:18,115
High risk. About a 28 percent chance of a flare-up in the next three days. And it explains why.

123
00:10:18,115 --> 00:10:23,789
Her breathing is 4 breaths above her own normal. Her oxygen is 2 points below it.

124
00:10:23,789 --> 00:10:28,132
She's hardly walking, and she's using her rescue inhaler more.

125
00:10:28,622 --> 00:10:31,410
That's the whole point of a personal twin.

126
00:10:31,410 --> 00:10:36,720
Compared with a textbook, she looks fine. Compared with herself, she's drifting.

127
00:10:36,720 --> 00:10:42,495
The twin also flags that COPD is rare under 30, so a doctor reads the result with care.

128
00:10:42,976 --> 00:10:50,373
We ran other tests too. Readings at her normal give about 0.3 percent. Strong warning signs give 99.

129
00:10:50,373 --> 00:10:55,477
And this table shows every value the twin used, so nothing is hidden.

130
00:10:58,631 --> 00:11:02,211
So what does the patient actually wear? Nothing unusual.

131
00:11:02,211 --> 00:11:05,343
No needle, no blood test, nothing under the skin.

132
00:11:05,343 --> 00:11:09,178
Just a smartwatch, a fingertip oximeter, or an inhaler clip.

133
00:11:09,672 --> 00:11:15,089
Here's what they'd see. These are design mock-ups, filled with a real patient's twin output.

134
00:11:15,089 --> 00:11:17,385
The watch shows today's risk as a ring.

135
00:11:17,385 --> 00:11:23,096
The phone app, My lungs today, says it in plain words, and shows the air for the next three days.

136
00:11:23,587 --> 00:11:29,137
For a high-risk patient, the watch gives one clear message. Your rescue inhaler use is up.

137
00:11:29,137 --> 00:11:32,344
A flare-up may be coming. Your doctor has been told.

138
00:11:32,806 --> 00:11:37,265
And if oxygen drops below 90? Simply: call your doctor now.

139
00:11:37,721 --> 00:11:42,608
There are three kits. The minimum is any smartwatch plus a fingertip oximeter.

140
00:11:42,608 --> 00:11:48,121
The full kit adds a smart inhaler clip, like ADHERO from Lupin and Aptar, sold in India.

141
00:11:48,121 --> 00:11:54,136
And for families who can't afford a watch, there's a no-watch kit: just a phone and an oximeter.

142
00:11:54,602 --> 00:11:58,855
To be clear: in this prototype, readings are simulated or typed in.

143
00:11:58,855 --> 00:12:05,075
These device connections are our integration plan, using each platform's published data interface.

144
00:12:08,252 --> 00:12:11,495
Now, the honest part. What's real, and what isn't?

145
00:12:11,990 --> 00:12:17,433
All the air quality and weather data is real. The training patients are synthetic.

146
00:12:17,433 --> 00:12:23,607
The health records come from Synthea, an open-source generator of synthetic hospital records,

147
00:12:23,607 --> 00:12:26,396
plus 120 younger Indian patients we added.

148
00:12:26,396 --> 00:12:30,445
The watch readings and flare-ups come from our own simulator,

149
00:12:30,445 --> 00:12:36,486
and every effect size in it comes from a published study, or is labelled as our assumption.

150
00:12:36,949 --> 00:12:37,873
Why simulate?

151
00:12:37,873 --> 00:12:45,053
Because we couldn't find any open dataset of COPD patients with wearable data and labelled flare-ups.

152
00:12:45,053 --> 00:12:48,323
That's the honest gap, and it's our next step.

153
00:12:48,798 --> 00:12:55,067
On 126 patients the model never saw, the accuracy score, AUROC, is 0.815.

154
00:12:55,067 --> 00:13:00,218
Published real-world COPD tools score between 0.68 and 0.85.

155
00:13:00,218 --> 00:13:06,487
The twin warned of two in every three flare-ups, one to three days ahead,

156
00:13:06,487 --> 00:13:14,644
with less than one false alert per patient per month. But remember: the patients are simulated.

157
00:13:14,644 --> 00:13:19,967
So this proves the pipeline works. It's not clinical accuracy.

158
00:13:20,420 --> 00:13:26,108
The chart also shows where the signal comes from. Wearables alone score 0.82.

159
00:13:26,108 --> 00:13:33,125
Air and weather alone, only 0.57. Air can't tell you which three days one person will flare up.

160
00:13:33,125 --> 00:13:40,364
It tells you when risk rises across a city and a season, and that's what powers the smog warnings.

161
00:13:40,830 --> 00:13:44,015
We also tested the twin on real people:

162
00:13:44,015 --> 00:13:50,877
100 de-identified hospital patients from the open MIMIC-III database, aged 18 to 90.

163
00:13:50,877 --> 00:13:55,206
It scored all 75 with enough data, with zero crashes.

164
00:13:55,206 --> 00:14:01,496
And its risk rose with real worsening signs, with a rank correlation of 0.73.

165
00:14:01,961 --> 00:14:09,309
And the limits? It didn't predict death in intensive care, which is a different event from a flare-up at home.

166
00:14:09,309 --> 00:14:16,657
Real hospital patients are sicker than our synthetic ones. And the groups are small. So this is a stress test.

167
00:14:16,657 --> 00:14:18,260
Not clinical validation.

168
00:14:21,417 --> 00:14:26,094
So who does this help? The doctor gets a ranked list of who to call, and why.

169
00:14:26,094 --> 00:14:31,560
The community health worker can follow hundreds of patients and visit the few who need it.

170
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:35,265
The family gets early warning, and plain advice on smog days.

171
00:14:35,715 --> 00:14:37,606
Here's our plan, in three phases.

172
00:14:38,056 --> 00:14:39,120
First, validate.

173
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:45,703
We want to partner with a pulmonology clinic and follow real COPD patients through one smog season.

174
00:14:45,703 --> 00:14:52,751
The test is simple: how many flare-ups does the twin catch early, and how many false alarms does it raise?

175
00:14:53,201 --> 00:14:54,770
Second, the patient app.

176
00:14:54,770 --> 00:15:00,656
A daily check-in that works with just a phone and an oximeter, alerts on SMS and WhatsApp,

177
00:15:00,656 --> 00:15:02,814
and Hindi and regional languages.

178
00:15:03,264 --> 00:15:04,306
And third, scale.

179
00:15:04,306 --> 00:15:10,622
Live air-quality forecasts, links to ABHA health records, and rollout through community health workers.

180
00:15:11,072 --> 00:15:16,001
One last thing. We're two MBA students, and neither of us writes code.

181
00:15:16,001 --> 00:15:23,042
We built BreatheTwin with an AI assistant, and put our effort into the questions a clinic would ask.

182
00:15:23,042 --> 00:15:29,942
Is it safe? Is it honest about its data? Would a doctor actually use it? And it's all open source.

183
00:15:30,392 --> 00:15:34,394
BreatheTwin. Early warning, easier breathing. Thank you!

184
00:15:34,844 --> 00:15:39,128
Thank you! The live demo and the code are linked in our submission.
